WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.601
-Our next guest is
an award-winning, best-selling

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.501
author and staff writer at
"The New Yorker."

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:07.434
His book, "Rogues: True Stories
of Grifters,

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:09.801
Killers, Rebels and Crooks"
is out today.

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:11.167
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:13.634
our friend Patrick Radden Keefe,
everybody.

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:15.501
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.501 --> 00:00:20.868
♪♪

00:00:20.868 --> 00:00:26.234
♪♪

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:28.501
Welcome back, Patrick!
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:00:28.501 --> 00:00:30.767
-It's great to be back.
-So this is a collection of

00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:32.567
fantastic stories you've written
over the years

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.167
for "The New Yorker"

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.701
and grifters, killers,
rebels and crooks --

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:40.300
Did you realize that your work
had this sort of thematic arc

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.367
of these kind of people?
-Not at the time.

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:44.701
I mean, the weird thing is,
I take some pride in not having

00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:46.434
a beat, and I think of myself
as just kind of

00:00:46.434 --> 00:00:48.467
zigging and zagging and
following interesting stories

00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:49.534
wherever they go.

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:50.767
And then when I put this book
together,

00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:53.367
it's 12 stories
over the course of 12 years.

00:00:53.367 --> 00:00:55.334
And I realized I just have like
two or three themes.

00:00:55.334 --> 00:00:57.634
I keep coming back, you know,
to these sort of

00:00:57.634 --> 00:01:00.934
big personalities who either are
pushing the limits of the law

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:02.834
or living just outside the law.

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:04.634
-You wrote a story about
El Chapo,

00:01:04.634 --> 00:01:07.234
and then El Chapo reached out.

00:01:07.234 --> 00:01:10.133
-Got in touch. Yeah. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:14.234
-But it's sort of a -- I should
note, wanted to collaborate.

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:16.467
-Yeah, it was a weird -- it was
a very strange scenario.

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:17.734
So occasionally, I'll write
these stories

00:01:17.734 --> 00:01:19.334
about people who won't
talk to me.

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:21.434
We call it a writearound, so
it's like, if I'm profiling you

00:01:21.434 --> 00:01:23.067
and you won't give me
an interview, I'll just

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:24.501
sort of write around you.

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:27.300
And I did that with El Chapo,
who is in prison in Mexico,

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.634
and he couldn't talk.

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:30.667
And so I came out with
this big profile.

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:33.734
And not long after, I got a call
from a guy who introduced

00:01:33.734 --> 00:01:35.367
himself as a lawyer
for El Chapo.

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:36.767
And I was just --
I was panicked

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.434
because it had not occurred
to me that he would --

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:41.434
He didn't seem like
a "New Yorker" subscriber.

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:42.934
I didn't think he would read --

00:01:42.934 --> 00:01:45.000
-Loves the cartoons.
-He only reads the cartoons.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.767
I figured he might have skipped
the article.

00:01:46.767 --> 00:01:48.868
And so I was just
kind of dissembling.

00:01:48.868 --> 00:01:50.033
I didn't know what to say
to this guy.

00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:51.467
And he said,
"We've read the article."

00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:54.634
And then he said, "El Senor" --
They call him "El Senor."

00:01:54.634 --> 00:01:58.234
He said, "El Senor is ready to
write his memoirs."

00:01:58.234 --> 00:02:00.767
And I was -- I had no idea
where this was going.

00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:03.901
And so I was just like, "That's
a book I'd like to read."

00:02:03.901 --> 00:02:05.567
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:08.767
And he said, "But, sir, is it
a book you would like to write?"

00:02:08.767 --> 00:02:10.801
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.801 --> 00:02:12.567
And so I was terrified.

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:16.033
I had to -- Before we got off
the phone, I had said "no"

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:18.167
because it just didn't seem like
a good idea.

00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:20.667
I felt like,
even in the best of scenarios,

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:22.033
you know, the ghost writer
and the sub--

00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:24.067
sometimes they don't get along,
it doesn't end well.

00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:26.133
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:28.334
-And what's funny is he called.

00:02:28.334 --> 00:02:29.734
So I said "no" on that call.

00:02:29.734 --> 00:02:31.400
He called a week later
and he said,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.400
"As you continue to consider
our offer --"

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.734
I was, "No, no! I've considered.
I've considered."

00:02:35.734 --> 00:02:38.734
But I didn't end up
writing that book.

00:02:38.734 --> 00:02:41.367
-You talk about, again,
as a "New Yorker" reporter,

00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:43.100
sometimes you got a writearound,
sometimes you're trying to

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.234
talk to subjects who are tricky.

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:46.501
They don't like talking to
reporters.

00:02:46.501 --> 00:02:47.634
They don't trust them.

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:49.734
Do you have some tricks
in your tool bag?

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:53.267
Have you had luck over the years
sort of developing a technique

00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:55.767
to get people to talk
who might otherwise not?

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:57.367
-You know, what I often tell
people is,

00:02:57.367 --> 00:02:59.467
"Listen, the train is
leaving the station.

00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:00.701
If you don't get on it,

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:02.200
that doesn't mean
it's not going to go.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.067
I'm going to write the piece
whether you cooperate or not."

00:03:04.067 --> 00:03:07.234
But the truth is that it's often
the case with some of

00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:08.834
these people that, even if you
get the interview,

00:03:08.834 --> 00:03:10.567
they're not going to tell you
anything all that helpful.

00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:13.234
I had this funny experience with
Gerry Adams,

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:15.868
who was in the Irish Republican
Army, the IRA.

00:03:15.868 --> 00:03:18.234
I wrote a book about them
and he wouldn't talk with me.

00:03:18.234 --> 00:03:20.467
And I remember just kind of
complaining and saying,

00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:21.968
"Oh, God, I wish he would talk."

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:24.801
And another guy who'd been in
the IRA said, "Oh, you know, he

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:26.868
would never tell you anything,
even if he did talk to you."

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:28.434
And I was sort of
insulted by this.

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:30.267
And I said, "Oh, you know, I've
got my -- I've got my ways.

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:32.133
I'm an experienced journalist.
I could probably get

00:03:32.133 --> 00:03:34.300
some good stuff
out of Gerry Adams."

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.067
And the guy kind of chuckled and
he said, "You know, Gerry's had

00:03:37.067 --> 00:03:39.501
what we call
counter-interrogation training,

00:03:39.501 --> 00:03:42.601
which means you could be
torturing him

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:44.133
and he wouldn't
tell you anything.

00:03:44.133 --> 00:03:46.667
But if you want to go in there
with your wee notebook..."

00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:49.501
[ Laughter ]
"...good luck to you."

00:03:49.501 --> 00:03:52.567
I'm never living this down.
Any time I'm working at home,

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:54.534
my wife will just kind of roll
her eyes and say,

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:56.534
"Your wee notebook.
Don't forget your wee notebook."

00:03:56.534 --> 00:03:58.467
[ Laughter ]
-That's very unfair.

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:02.934
-Yeah.
-You know, you write nonfiction,

00:04:02.934 --> 00:04:04.300
but I feel like you write --

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.934
and again, you write
about big characters.

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:09.534
You know, you wrote about the
Sacklers in the opioid crisis.

00:04:09.534 --> 00:04:11.767
You wrote about the IRA.

00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:14.434
It strikes me that one of the
really effective ways you write

00:04:14.434 --> 00:04:16.300
is -- obviously,
it's nonfiction,

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.701
but it reads like a story.

00:04:17.701 --> 00:04:20.434
Is that something
that you set out to do?

00:04:20.434 --> 00:04:22.267
Like, to create the characters
in a way

00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:23.701
that almost feels like a novel?

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:25.100
-I'm so glad to
hear you say that

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.200
if only because I -- I mean,
that's very much by design.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.334
I grew up in a family, like, an
Irish-American family in Boston

00:04:31.334 --> 00:04:32.334
where everybody's telling
stories --

00:04:32.334 --> 00:04:36.300
[ Man cheers ]
Alright! Boston.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.000
Where people are telling stories
and retelling stories.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.734
And, you know, it's considered
kind of a skill to tell

00:04:41.734 --> 00:04:44.868
and retell a story, which I
realize not everybody finds

00:04:44.868 --> 00:04:47.000
tremendously amusing.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.734
And so some of it is -- yeah,
it's about, you know, I start

00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:51.634
books all the time
that I don't finish.

00:04:51.634 --> 00:04:53.834
And so some of it is about
trying to kind of write

00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:56.701
these stories in a way that'll
pull you in and hold on to you

00:04:56.701 --> 00:04:58.200
and hopefully you'll get
right to the end.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.968
-So you've written
about the IRA.

00:04:59.968 --> 00:05:01.801
You've written
about the opioid crisis.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.734
This is "Grifters, Killers,
Rebels and Crooks."

00:05:03.734 --> 00:05:05.133
Kids book next, do you think?

00:05:05.133 --> 00:05:06.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:08.701
-I thought maybe a book on yoga
or meditation.

00:05:08.701 --> 00:05:12.200
[ Laughter ]
Or like microdosing thing?

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.701
-You and Kesha should write
something.

00:05:13.701 --> 00:05:15.834
She's got a real trip of
an experience right now.

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:17.901
-I'm going to approach her
backstage.

00:05:17.901 --> 00:05:20.033
-Go ghost hunting with Kesha.
I think you'll enjoy it.

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:21.467
Hey, congratulations
on the book.

00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:23.400
It's so great to see you.
Patrick Radden Keefe, everybody.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.534
"Rogues" is available now.

00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:26.634
We'll be right back.

